Patch 62 -- the island's side of the GL-slot trap, and glDrawBuffer resolved.

Island-only, and verified so: the guest i386 `utils.o` is BYTE-IDENTICAL with and
without this patch, whole object including DWARF, because everything here is
inside the trailing `#ifdef MGS2_ISLAND_ARM` block and nothing after it uses
`__LINE__`.

That verification is why the change lives here. The first attempt put the GLES
mapping at the call site in `context_gl.c`, and the 30-line comment shifted every
`checkGLcall()` line number after it -- the guest `.text` then differed in the
`__LINE__` immediates baked into GL-error diagnostics. Inert, but it would have
cost the "island patches leave the shipping DLL untouched" invariant for nothing.

## 1. Unresolved slots get a naming stub instead of NULL

A slot that cannot be resolved now gets `mgs2_island_gl_trap(slot)` -- a stub that
prints the slot, its name and its caller, then aborts (Box86 patch 10). In a
production build that helper returns NULL, so the fail-closed behaviour is
unchanged. `mgs2_island_gl_slot_name()` is added here because this file owns the
macro-generated name lists, and one numbering now covers every table it
translates:

```text
0                     .. count-1   gl_ops, position IS the name
count                 .. +20       fbo_ops
count + 0x100         .. +N        ffp_attrib_ops.generic[]
count + 0x200 / +0x201             glDisableWINE / glEnableWINE
```

## 2. glDrawBuffer is implemented, not faked

The trap named the blocker in one run: slot 90, `glDrawBuffer`, called from
`wined3d_context_gl_apply_fbo_state+0x2a0` -- the inlined
`wined3d_context_gl_set_draw_buffer(context_gl, GL_NONE)` on the FBO attach path.

The scalar entry point does not exist anywhere on this stack:

```text
libmali-bifrost-g52    exports glDrawBuffers and glReadBuffer, NO glDrawBuffer
                       (readelf --dyn-syms on the device)
Wine's GLES path       has no USE_GL_FUNC(glDrawBuffer) in win32u, so the unix
                       half of the guest's own slot is not filled either
```

So it is not a resolution failure to be fixed by looking harder. The island
installs its own implementation: `glDrawBuffers(1, &buffer)`, which is the
documented GLES equivalent and, more to the point, the mapping THIS TREE ALREADY
USES for the same reason in `wined3d_context_gl_apply_draw_buffers()`.

It is installed only if `glDrawBuffers` itself resolved, and it is reported
separately -- "substituted natively", not "resolved" -- because a substitution and
a resolution are different facts:

```text
MGS2 island: GL slot 90 (glDrawBuffer) substituted natively: glDrawBuffers(1, &buffer)
```

No no-op, no faked success, nothing that returns a plausible value: an
unimplementable call still has to fail loudly.

OPEN QUESTION, deliberately left open: whether the GUEST ever executes that line.
If it does, it would call through a null unix pointer, which production does not
do -- so either it always takes the early return above it, or something outside
this tree fills that pointer. A memory-only counter on the guest side would
settle it. Until then this is an island-side resolution, not a renderer change.

RESULT. Entry 34 no longer traps and no longer raises an illegal instruction on
a GL pointer. It now gets far enough to reach DirectMusic startup, and stops on
the NEXT layer: a guest wined3d function pointer that no instrumented dispatch
site covers -- `convert_b8g8r8a8_unorm_gles`, reached through the format-converter
pointer. Section 19 of the brief.

NOT MEASURED. Diagnostic path only.

